WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:16.680
I was born in Hamilton, Ontario, Canada.

00:00:16.690 --> 00:00:19.300
But I grew up mostly in Long Island, New York.

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:22.460
My ancestral homeland is in Jaipur, Rajasthan,
India.

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:24.939
There are a lot of academics in my family.

00:00:24.939 --> 00:00:29.860
My mother is a mathematician, and my grandfather
was a professor of Sanskrit and ancient Indian

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:30.860
history.

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:34.890
A lot of mathematics I learned as a child
was through what he taught me about the rhythms

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:36.600
of Sanskrit poetry.

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:42.980
I think mathematicians do mathematics for
reasons that are very similar to those of

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:46.650
musicians playing music, or any artist doing
their art.

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:52.700
It's all about trying to contribute to a certain
understanding of ourselves and the world around us.

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:59.860
One key to solving hard mathematical problems
that people have been thinking about for many

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:04.150
years, is to think about them in a totally
different way.

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:08.430
One of the most exciting moments of discovery
for me occurred when I was a graduate student

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:09.430
at Princeton.

00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:11.750
Those days I had been thinking about Gauss
composition.

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:17.540
So Gauss composition is a method by which
one can take two quadratic expressions and

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:22.240
two variables and combine them to produce
a third such quadratic expression.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:26.660
At one moment it just clicked — it was around
midnight, when I usually go to sleep — that

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:30.920
if I take a Rubik's cube and I cut off the
top layer and then I put numbers in the remaining

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:35.760
little cubes, that that would lead to a cubic
analogue of Gauss composition.

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:42.440
I went on to discover 12 more analogues of
Gauss composition over the next couple years,

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:44.999
but that first one suddenly opened this door.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:48.700
And that door was opened in part because I
was playing with toys.

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:55.080
A lot of the work I've done over the past
several years has involved developing techniques

00:01:55.080 --> 00:02:00.610
in the geometry of numbers in order to solve
problems about the number of number fields

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:06.989
having bounded discriminate or the number
of rational points on random elliptic curves

00:02:06.989 --> 00:02:08.890
or higher genius curves.

00:02:08.890 --> 00:02:14.130
These proofs about the existence or non-existence
of rational solutions to elliptic curve equations

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:20.030
have in particular now led to a proof, which
is joint work with Chris Skinner and Wei Zhang,

00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:24.830
that most elliptic curves satisfy the Birch
and Swinnerton-Dyer conjecture.

00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:29.080
Some of the most applicable mathematics that's
occurred in history has been discovered not

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:33.600
for the purpose of the application but because
one was just searching for the most beautiful

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:34.540
mathematics out there.

